1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,885
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,885 --> 00:00:06,680
This is chapter twenty-eight: executors.

3
00:00:07,130 --> 00:00:11,073
An executor separates what to run from which thread runs it.

4
00:00:11,073 --> 00:00:16,724
But the convenient factory methods have defaults that cause real production incidents.

5
00:00:16,724 --> 00:00:17,775
Let's see three.

6
00:00:18,675 --> 00:00:19,745
Three incidents.

7
00:00:19,745 --> 00:00:28,098
A fixed thread pool sits on an unbounded queue, so when producers outpace the workers, the queue grows until memory runs out.

8
00:00:28,098 --> 00:00:35,248
A cached thread pool has unbounded threads, so a burst of slow tasks creates thousands of platform threads.

9
00:00:35,248 --> 00:00:41,663
And a scheduled job threw an exception once, which cancelled every future run, with no log line.

10
00:00:41,663 --> 00:00:43,401
Nobody noticed for a week.

11
00:00:43,851 --> 00:00:48,032
Each one is the factory method working exactly as documented.

12
00:00:48,932 --> 00:00:53,391
ThreadPoolExecutor has a core size, a maximum size, and a queue.

13
00:00:53,391 --> 00:00:58,617
When a task arrives, if fewer than core threads are running, it starts one.

14
00:00:59,067 --> 00:01:01,350
Otherwise it tries to queue the task.

15
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:06,707
Only if the queue refuses does it start extra threads, up to the maximum.

16
00:01:07,157 --> 00:01:09,525
And past that, it rejects the task.

17
00:01:09,975 --> 00:01:13,910
Most people assume it grows to the maximum before queuing.

18
00:01:13,910 --> 00:01:15,131
It's the opposite.

19
00:01:15,131 --> 00:01:20,966
So with an unbounded queue, the queue never refuses, and the maximum is never reached.

20
00:01:20,966 --> 00:01:28,769
A pool with core ten, max a hundred, and an unbounded queue runs exactly ten threads forever while its queue grows.

21
00:01:29,669 --> 00:01:34,324
For a production service, construct ThreadPoolExecutor directly.

22
00:01:34,324 --> 00:01:40,432
Core equals max at the core count, because CPU work gains nothing from more threads.

23
00:01:40,432 --> 00:01:42,469
A bounded queue caps memory.

24
00:01:42,469 --> 00:01:46,178
A named thread factory makes thread dumps readable.

25
00:01:46,178 --> 00:01:50,832
availableProcessors respects container CPU limits since Java 10.

26
00:01:51,282 --> 00:01:53,441
And choose a rejection policy.

27
00:01:53,441 --> 00:01:54,808
The default throws.

28
00:01:54,808 --> 00:01:57,687
The discard policies silently lose work.

29
00:01:57,687 --> 00:02:08,050
CallerRunsPolicy is the useful one: when the pool is full, the submitting thread runs the task itself, which slows producers down automatically.

30
00:02:08,950 --> 00:02:15,775
For I/O-bound work, the classic sizing formula says cores times one plus wait over compute.

31
00:02:15,775 --> 00:02:23,350
Eight cores, five milliseconds of compute, ninety-five of waiting, gives a hundred and sixty threads.

32
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:26,293
On Java 21 and later, don't do that.

33
00:02:26,293 --> 00:02:31,627
Use virtual threads, and bound the real constraint directly with a Semaphore.

34
00:02:31,627 --> 00:02:38,345
The formula was always an indirect way of expressing a downstream limit through thread economics.

35
00:02:38,345 --> 00:02:44,579
A semaphore says it directly, and virtual threads remove the reason thread count mattered.

36
00:02:45,479 --> 00:02:46,989
Where do exceptions go?

37
00:02:46,989 --> 00:02:53,622
With execute, an exception reaches the thread's uncaught exception handler, so it's logged, at least.

38
00:02:53,622 --> 00:03:02,422
With submit, it's captured inside the returned Future, and if you discard that Future, which happens a lot, the failure is never seen.

39
00:03:02,422 --> 00:03:10,631
So consume every Future with get and a timeout, use execute for fire-and-forget work, or wrap tasks in a try-catch that logs.

40
00:03:11,531 --> 00:03:13,696
Here's the silent schedule fix.

41
00:03:13,696 --> 00:03:22,567
If any run of a scheduled task throws, every later run is suppressed, and the exception sits in a ScheduledFuture nobody holds.

42
00:03:22,567 --> 00:03:28,853
So catch everything inside the task, log it, and the schedule survives to retry next time.

43
00:03:29,303 --> 00:03:41,037
And know the difference between fixed rate, which is start to start, so slow runs bunch up, and fixed delay, which guarantees a gap between the end of one run and the start of the next.

44
00:03:41,937 --> 00:03:42,958
Use bulkheads.

45
00:03:42,958 --> 00:03:51,641
Give each downstream dependency its own pool, so a slow vendor API saturates the vendor pool and payments keep flowing.

46
00:03:51,641 --> 00:03:57,041
With virtual threads, you get the same isolation from separate semaphores.

47
00:03:57,491 --> 00:03:59,162
And shut down properly.

48
00:03:59,162 --> 00:04:01,343
shutdown stops accepting work.

49
00:04:01,343 --> 00:04:07,376
Await termination with a timeout, then shutdownNow, which interrupts running tasks.

50
00:04:07,376 --> 00:04:10,865
That only helps if they respond to interruption.

51
00:04:10,865 --> 00:04:15,517
Since Java 19, short-lived executors work in try-with-resources.

52
00:04:16,417 --> 00:04:30,796
Inside ThreadPoolExecutor, a field called ctl packs the run state and the worker count into one atomic integer, so both are checked and updated in a single compare-and-swap, with no global lock.

53
00:04:31,246 --> 00:04:34,443
Each worker loops, taking tasks from the queue.

54
00:04:34,443 --> 00:04:39,339
Non-core threads exit after sitting idle, which is how the pool shrinks.

55
00:04:39,339 --> 00:04:44,440
And a task that throws through execute kills its worker, which is replaced.

56
00:04:44,440 --> 00:04:48,249
Harmless, but failing tasks cause constant thread churn.

57
00:04:48,699 --> 00:04:54,572
And the cached pool explosion comes from SynchronousQueue, which has no capacity at all.

58
00:04:54,572 --> 00:05:01,179
If no thread is idle, the offer fails, and the pool starts a new thread, potentially without limit.

59
00:05:02,079 --> 00:05:03,318
Production rules.

60
00:05:03,318 --> 00:05:09,660
Construct ThreadPoolExecutor directly, with a bounded queue and a named thread factory.

61
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:12,430
Use CallerRunsPolicy for backpressure.

62
00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:15,710
Use virtual threads with a semaphore for I/O.

63
00:05:15,710 --> 00:05:18,918
Put a try-catch inside every scheduled task.

64
00:05:18,918 --> 00:05:21,833
And bulkhead each downstream dependency.

65
00:05:22,283 --> 00:05:26,435
Never use the fixed or cached factory methods in a service.

66
00:05:26,435 --> 00:05:29,038
Never discard the Future from submit.

67
00:05:29,038 --> 00:05:33,542
Never expect the maximum size to matter with an unbounded queue.

68
00:05:33,542 --> 00:05:36,708
Never share one pool across every dependency.

69
00:05:36,708 --> 00:05:39,030
And never skip a proper shutdown.

70
00:05:39,930 --> 00:05:40,574
To recap.

71
00:05:40,574 --> 00:05:46,015
A pool fills core threads, then the queue, then extra threads, then rejects.

72
00:05:46,015 --> 00:05:50,883
So bound the queue, name the threads, and choose a rejection policy.

73
00:05:50,883 --> 00:05:53,890
submit hides exceptions inside the Future.

74
00:05:53,890 --> 00:05:58,114
One exception cancels a schedule, so catch inside the task.

75
00:05:58,114 --> 00:06:03,269
And for I/O-bound work on Java 21, use virtual threads with a semaphore.

76
00:06:03,719 --> 00:06:06,846
Next is chapter twenty-nine: CompletableFuture.

77
00:06:06,846 --> 00:06:08,177
Thanks for watching.
